WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.908 --> 00:00:02.820
- [Interviewer] Okay, Audy.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.170
Audy, where are you from originally?

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.211
Where'd you grow up?

00:00:05.211 --> 00:00:10.190
- [Audy] I growed up in Indiana
till I was eight year old.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.750
And then my dad retired and moved back

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.341
to his home place in
Leslie County, Kentucky.

00:00:16.341 --> 00:00:18.510
- [Interviewer] And tell
me about your childhood.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:21.560
You had both your parents
growing up as a kid?

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.320
- Up till I was 12 year old, 11 year old.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.072
Then my mom and dad separated.

00:00:27.072 --> 00:00:29.170
- [Interviewer] And who raised you then?

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:32.470
- My dad, and I stayed
with my mom sometimes,

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:36.967
but not much, because I started
going down the wrong path.

00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:39.390
- [Interviewer] What kind of
stuff were you getting into?

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:44.390
- Drugs. Smoking weed, and
doing pill called Xanax

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:47.054
and Percocet.

00:00:47.054 --> 00:00:48.320
- [Interviewer] Oh, really?

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.153
- Yeah.

00:00:49.153 --> 00:00:50.440
- [Interviewer] How old are you?

00:00:51.356 --> 00:00:52.607
- 46 year old.

00:00:52.607 --> 00:00:53.440
- [Interviewer] 46?

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:54.984
- Be 46 in January, the 19th.

00:00:54.984 --> 00:00:56.230
- [Interviewer] So that
was kind of the beginning

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:59.499
of the drug problem here in Appalachia?

00:00:59.499 --> 00:01:00.332
- Yeah.

00:01:01.344 --> 00:01:03.260
- [Interviewer] How far
did you go in school?

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.170
- Eighth grade.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.800
- [Interviewer] Eighth grade?

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.908
And did you go to work
at some point there?

00:01:07.908 --> 00:01:09.973
- Yeah. I worked all my life.

00:01:09.973 --> 00:01:14.333
Out of these hills, I worked
a few tax-paying jobs.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.240
But I dig roots called yam, cohosh,

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:24.383
we call it rattle weed, and stone root.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.016
In the winter-time now.

00:01:27.016 --> 00:01:27.853
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:30.343
- In the summer I dig
ginseng and yellow root.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:33.678
And strip elm trees.

00:01:33.678 --> 00:01:34.900
- [Interviewer] Of their bark?

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.630
- On private property. Yeah.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.550
People let's us have them cause
they're no good for lumber.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.430
You can't saw them up for lumber.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:42.680
- [Interviewer] But the bark is?

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:43.990
- The bark is valuable.

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.514
- [Interviewer] Yeah. And
you, like this ginseng,

00:01:46.514 --> 00:01:48.288
you sell to Chinese?

00:01:48.288 --> 00:01:50.228
- Well, I sell them to
local dealers around here.

00:01:50.228 --> 00:01:52.020
- [Interviewer] To a middle man.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.560
- Ever who pays the most, you know,

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.850
that's who I sell to.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.930
And that's what they do with it.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:58.330
They sell it to the Chinese.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.510
And that's where your most money is,

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:04.713
is in ginseng and yellow root.

00:02:07.056 --> 00:02:10.281
- [Interviewer] And this is,
the life here in Appalachia,

00:02:10.281 --> 00:02:13.610
especially in the mountains,
it's a tough life, right?

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:14.729
It's a difficult way to survive.

00:02:14.729 --> 00:02:16.000
- Yeah. It's real tough.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.370
Cause the way I done dig,
I'd have to take a mattock

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.907
and dig these out of
the ground, one by one.

00:02:23.448 --> 00:02:27.600
And when they're just worth a
couple cents a root, you know,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.183
it takes a lot of roots
to make a hundred dollars.

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:32.800
It's a lot of digging,

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.855
that's two good sacks
is a hundred dollars.

00:02:36.855 --> 00:02:39.510
I'm talking about sacks, like this.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.610
You can imagine how many
roots this round will go

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:43.470
into one sack.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.733
That be, probably three,
400 roots, if not more.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:52.657
And that's three or 400 times,
I have to dig a hole down

00:02:52.657 --> 00:02:55.488
in the ground to get it out.

00:02:55.488 --> 00:02:56.790
So it's rough.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:57.643
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.890
Do you love your life here in Appalachia?

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:01.997
- I love it.

00:03:01.997 --> 00:03:04.900
- [Interviewer] What are your
favorite things about it?

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.500
- The hills is my number
one favorite thing about it

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:13.000
and being able to go in
and out of them, you know,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.890
when I want to, 'cause they
got diamond trails and stuff.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:18.563
We dig roots out them too.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:20.880
It's for water trails.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.880
It goes for miles and
miles through these hills.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:28.880
And I ride my four-wheeler
along there and I'll stop.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.910
And like a swag, we
call it or a small hole.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:35.770
Go down in 'em, look for roots.

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.330
You know, to me every day I have to work,

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:42.160
just about every day to survive.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.730
And know I've done everything
in the world to survive.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.200
I've sold pills, I've sold pot.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.020
But all that's behind me.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:51.563
I don't do that crap no more.

00:03:52.399 --> 00:03:55.530
I just work for everything I get now,

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.880
easy money is never good money.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:03:59.278
- [Interviewer] And what
are the worst things

00:03:59.278 --> 00:04:00.157
about living here?

00:04:00.157 --> 00:04:01.433
What's the hardest part?

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:03.850
Just that you have to work so much?

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:04.750
- 'Cause, there ain't no jobs,

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:06.990
you know, no good paying jobs.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.720
If you get it sure, you have
to have a house or you know

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.940
like working out the
hills or you may work,

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:14.100
be a carpenter or something.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.427
I can get odd jobs and just
enough to feed yourself with

00:04:17.427 --> 00:04:19.310
is about how you make.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:20.370
- [Interviewer] Mm-hmm. I mean,

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.070
it's one of the poorest
parts of the country.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:22.903
- Yeah.

00:04:22.903 --> 00:04:24.540
- [Interviewer] And everyone
is going through the same,

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:25.510
a similar struggle.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:26.343
- Yeah.

00:04:26.343 --> 00:04:27.380
- [Interviewer] Is that
what makes everyone

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:28.520
so helpful to each other?

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.720
- Yeah. Somebody ain't
got a place to stay.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.640
I've took a many a person in my home.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.970
And then you didn't see my
home, I got a tiny home.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.140
You see them tiny homes that's on TV.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:44.370
I see that and I wanted
me and my wife to have one

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:46.930
and that's what I'd done.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:51.000
I'm still working on it
slowly, but I'll get there.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.558
As I get the money, you know,
most days I just make 30,

00:04:55.558 --> 00:04:57.730
$40 a day, you know.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:00.300
But there's some days
I can make a hundred.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.133
At the end of the week,

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:02.470
I guess it averages up

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:05.273
to a couple hundred dollars, 250 a week.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:07.940
If we have a decent week.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.470
Now the bark pulling,

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:12.437
you can only do it three
months out of a year.

00:05:12.437 --> 00:05:14.200
Now that's real good money.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:17.650
You can make up or $500 a
week, but it's just for that

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.750
short period of time
that still don't help you

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:21.303
the rest of the year.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.643
And when it's 20 degrees out

00:05:24.643 --> 00:05:27.920
and you have to get up
at 5:30 in the morning,

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:31.748
it's still dark and get on
a cold four-wheeler ATV.

00:05:31.748 --> 00:05:34.320
You know, that's what we go to work on,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:39.320
you can't get a vehicle
or we go and it's rough.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:44.390
You know, I can't afford no side beside

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.869
or I could get something
that had a little heat in it.

00:05:47.869 --> 00:05:49.119
You know, then.

00:05:50.363 --> 00:05:52.690
- [Interviewer] So to heat
your home, you use what?

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:54.240
- I use propane.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:59.240
I can get a voucher down here
that helps me out with it.

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:01.144
And at LKLB.

00:06:01.144 --> 00:06:02.915
- [Interviewer] Have you traveled,

00:06:02.915 --> 00:06:04.093
have you been around the country?

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.773
- Yeah, I've been to Chicago,
you know, Ohio, Indiana.

00:06:10.733 --> 00:06:14.426
And this place you like asked, you know.

00:06:14.426 --> 00:06:16.676
- [Interviewer] Do you
ever consider leaving?

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:23.130
- Yeah, I probably
would if I had a vehicle

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.500
and could get a insurance on it.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:28.040
Yeah, I'd go where there was good jobs at,

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.280
that way I know I'm
making five or six a week.

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:34.486
My brother, he made
good money out his life

00:06:34.486 --> 00:06:37.230
and well he had a heart
attack and had to quit.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.170
He ain't much older than
me, just five years older.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:43.210
But the coal mines, they make
a old man out of a young man,

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.283
you know, he gets old before his time.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.072
Yeah. I most definitely growed up hard.

00:06:50.072 --> 00:06:52.660
- [Interviewer] Yeah.
Do you have children?

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:53.493
- No.

00:06:53.493 --> 00:06:54.326
- [Interviewer] No kids.

00:06:54.326 --> 00:06:55.159
- No, no children.

00:06:55.159 --> 00:06:56.042
- [Interviewer] If you did, would you -

00:06:56.042 --> 00:06:57.702
- See yeah, well I got step children,

00:06:57.702 --> 00:07:01.020
I got a little step-grand boy
I play with almost every day.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.920
You know, every day he comes out.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:04.390
- [Interviewer] And what do
you think about his future?

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:05.240
If he stays here?

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:09.880
- I think he will get, you
know, my nephew's stuff there.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:14.540
They all got like two,
maybe three college degrees.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:19.100
Well, my one nephew does,
he's a mortician and a what?

00:07:20.547 --> 00:07:22.773
A registered nurse,
national nurse practitioner.

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:25.530
- [Interviewer] But he stayed here?

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:29.260
- Yeah, but he might go to another county.

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:33.040
Where 'cause the nurse
right now or that Obamacare,

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:37.200
one reason nurses jobs
good to have right now.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.033
They give him,

00:07:38.033 --> 00:07:41.773
like a one place offered him
10,000 bonus to sign, you know?

00:07:41.773 --> 00:07:46.360
And that's just 10,000
and sign with them for

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:50.002
I guess so many years, you
know, sign a contract with them.

00:07:50.002 --> 00:07:53.073
Ask me he's his best offer,
at $30 they're starting out.

00:07:54.779 --> 00:07:57.540
I just don't got the education for it.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:00.135
I was in special-ed and never
could learn to read and write.

00:08:00.135 --> 00:08:01.240
- [Interviewer] Mm-hmm.

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:03.270
- So I had to get smart in other ways.

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:04.516
Street smart.

00:08:04.516 --> 00:08:07.120
- [Interviewer] Do you
consider yourself a hillbilly?

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.521
- Oh yeah. I'm hillbilly to the bone.

00:08:09.521 --> 00:08:11.830
All my people that did live in Indiana,

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:14.060
here's where they come from.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:15.720
You know, it was Leslie County.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:16.660
- [Interviewer] And you're proud of that?

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:17.590
- Yeah. I'm proud of it.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:19.000
- [Interviewer] Yeah. It's a-

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.640
- 'Cause we, it was just like at Hank

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.370
when we was juniors, home.

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.227
You know a country boy can survive

00:08:24.227 --> 00:08:27.380
and these country boys
will and can survive.

00:08:29.407 --> 00:08:30.730
- [Interviewer] And you're happy here?

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:32.453
- [Audy] Oh, I love it.

00:08:32.453 --> 00:08:34.840
Won't be nowhere but the mountains.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:36.130
- [Interviewer] Do you have any regrets?

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:37.230
- [Audy] Now, huh? No.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:40.090
- [Interviewer] And do you
feel like you've lived the life

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:40.923
that you wanted to?

00:08:40.923 --> 00:08:42.550
- [Audy] Yeah. Oh, I've loved my life.

00:08:42.550 --> 00:08:46.410
I love it 'cause I met my
wife and I was married to her.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:48.505
She passed away a year and half ago.

00:08:48.505 --> 00:08:50.250
- [Interviewer] Oh, I'm sorry.

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:54.343
- [Audy] And her name was Reva Jean Kelly.

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:58.028
That was the best thing happened to me,

00:08:58.028 --> 00:09:00.266
it helped get me off drugs and stuff.

00:09:00.266 --> 00:09:01.710
- [Interviewer] Oh she did?

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:06.410
- [Audy] Yeah, you know,
that's the reason I quit,

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:07.680
was for her.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:11.740
Cause you know, I've said it's my fault

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.409
that she was on 'em.

00:09:13.409 --> 00:09:14.720
Cause you know how you act.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:18.440
If you're doing something
most time in here,

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:22.073
in this country part,
they want to do it too.

00:09:22.073 --> 00:09:24.080
- [Interviewer] Yeah. So you started it,

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.782
you got her getting used to it?

00:09:25.782 --> 00:09:27.282
- Kel was already get started,

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.050
but that ain't what killed her.

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:32.840
You know what killed her?

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.050
She drunk bad water or
eat some type of bad meat.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:37.440
And it gave her a brain disease.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:41.550
They said that only 600
people in the world got it.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:44.490
You know, around the world
most time it's not here,

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:46.710
it's in a foreign country or something

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:48.563
where they have to drink bad water.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:52.950
- [Interviewer] And
that's what killed her?

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:55.382
- [Audy] UK hospital told us that anyway.

00:09:55.382 --> 00:09:56.460
- [Interviewer] That's what killed her?

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:57.418
- Yeah. That's what killed her.

00:09:57.418 --> 00:09:58.520
- [Interviewer] I'm sorry.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:01.310
- She was just as good
as we are right now.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:04.593
And five minutes later, we'd laid down,

00:10:04.593 --> 00:10:06.170
she tapped me on the shoulder

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:07.920
and said her right arm didn't move.

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.990
And then from there, she
just got worser every day,

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:13.643
five weeks, she was dead.

00:10:15.107 --> 00:10:17.530
Them spots had moved to her motor skills

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.020
and parts of her brain.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:20.750
I guess it worked her motor skills

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.653
and she didn't have no
control over her arms or legs.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:28.630
I tried to give her medicine.

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:33.240
It helped some kept her
from, her arms from jerking

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:35.590
and her legs would just kick like that.

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:38.883
You know, she was in bad shape.

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:41.770
Hated seeing her go like that.

00:10:44.251 --> 00:10:46.250
- [Interviewer] What's
the most important lesson

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:47.650
you've learned in your life?

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:50.780
- Don't do drugs.

00:10:50.780 --> 00:10:53.580
That's the message I can send to anybody,

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:56.900
that if they watch this, don't do drugs.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:58.940
If you just starting, quit now

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:01.600
'cause they ruin your life.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:02.760
I could had anything I wanted,

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.423
if I wasn't been a drug addict.

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:07.780
Could've lived in a brick home right now

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:09.843
and had new vehicles sitting in the yard.

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:13.080
'Cause I woulda had a good job

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.010
but I wasn't have been a drug addict.

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:16.543
And I wasted a lot of money.

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:23.010
I'm a little crippled up now from car.

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:28.010
I got three bad ricks and that
last rick wasn't long ago,

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:31.870
I ricked the forward broke my
back, both sides and my ribs.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:36.870
And when I'd pee, I'd pee
that blood and that was,

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:41.120
that's been right before
I went cold, sober.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:43.550
you know, that's what,

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:46.490
another thing that made me go cold, sober.

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:49.663
I made three times that I
got in a real bad accident.

